This has made me think that more model tech reports need to report the cost to run benchmarks, or maybe the compute used to achieve a given score. A lot can be hidden in just reporting throughput, latency, and benchmarks.

Test time compute is a great way to get better results, but if you need significantly more generation to match a larger model, it isn't as obvious of a win. You can also run into context rot problems if every step requires much more generation.

@code_star: "I tried reimplementing the DeepSeek hyperparameter calculator web app I made in my Substack article by giving GPT-5.5 a screenshot and a description of what it should do. Then, I had GPT-5.5, DeepSeekV4 Pro, and Kimi 2.6 attempt to implement it. Despite having very different token prices, they all completed at incredibly close final costs (about $1.20 – $1.50). Ironically, DeepSeekV4 Pro couldn't get it to work in a single shot."

I was trying to make sense of the 6% pretraining cost numbers. The answer was actually more shocking to me than I expected. This has got to be the largest, most expensive Once-For-All training run or implementation ever, right? It's a crazy week for all these throwback techniques from a few years ago taking center stage again.

So admittedly, the 6% figure hides the fact that it’s basically a pruned and distilled model, but in some ways, it’s actually way cooler that they were able to achieve this level of quality. What's the next deep learning trick that you haven’t seen in a while that’s going to make a comeback? Dropout?

